WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.060
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:15.309
Unit eighteen: Intertextuality. Today you will show alternatives with 'whether … or' clauses.

4
00:00:15.609 --> 00:00:18.238
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.317
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:24.684
Intertextuality.

7
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:29.517
Intertextuality means the way texts refer to and shape each other.

8
00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:33.417
Intertextuality enriches the film for attentive viewers.

9
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:37.041
Your turn. Say: Intertextuality.

10
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.400
Intertextuality.

11
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.116
Allusion.

12
00:00:44.117 --> 00:00:47.788
Allusion means an indirect reference to another work or event.

13
00:00:48.383 --> 00:00:50.789
The title is an allusion to a famous poem.

14
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.012
Your turn. Say: Allusion.

15
00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:57.914
Allusion.

16
00:00:58.650 --> 00:00:59.651
Reference.

17
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.102
Reference means a direct mention of something.

18
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:06.157
The song makes a clear reference to the Bible.

19
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.031
Your turn. Say: Reference.

20
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:12.937
Reference.

21
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:14.568
Homage.

22
00:01:15.583 --> 00:01:19.150
Homage means a respectful tribute to an earlier artist or work.

23
00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.202
The scene is an homage to Charlie Chaplin.

24
00:01:22.817 --> 00:01:25.179
Your turn. Say: Homage.

25
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:28.849
Homage.

26
00:01:29.583 --> 00:01:30.352
Parody.

27
00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:34.778
Parody means an imitation that mocks or comments on the original.

28
00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:37.731
The sketch is a parody of a cooking show.

29
00:01:38.317 --> 00:01:40.957
Your turn. Say: Parody.

30
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:44.673
Parody.

31
00:01:45.417 --> 00:01:46.151
Echo.

32
00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.798
Echo means a subtle repetition of an earlier text or idea.

33
00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:54.045
Her opening line is an echo of a fairy tale.

34
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.966
Your turn. Say: Echo.

35
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:00.604
Echo.

36
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.024
Dialogue between texts.

37
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:08.710
Dialogue between texts means the conversation texts create by referring to each other.

38
00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:12.129
Adaptations create a dialogue between texts.

39
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:15.950
Your turn. Say: Dialogue between texts.

40
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.869
Dialogue between texts.

41
00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:22.676
Layered meaning.

42
00:02:23.683 --> 00:02:27.006
Layered meaning means several levels of meaning in one text.

43
00:02:27.617 --> 00:02:29.941
The cartoon has layered meaning for adults.

44
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.226
Your turn. Say: Layered meaning.

45
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:37.079
Layered meaning.

46
00:02:37.867 --> 00:02:41.668
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.517 --> 00:02:47.944
Visal, you said the animated film you watched is full of intertextuality. What did you notice?

48
00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:58.236
Whether through allusion or parody, nearly every scene refers to an older film, from silent comedies to famous horror movies.

49
00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.361
Did the children in the cinema notice?

50
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:10.942
Probably not. But whether they recognised them or not, the scenes still worked, which is why the film has such layered meaning.

51
00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.502
Is a parody disrespectful to the original?

52
00:03:15.617 --> 00:03:24.274
It depends. Whether a reference is mockery or homage often depends on tone. This film clearly loved the films it borrowed from.

53
00:03:25.433 --> 00:03:26.992
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.442
Grammar focus: Whetheror clauses.

55
00:03:30.742 --> 00:03:32.045
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:36.038
Whether through allusion or parody, texts reference each other.

57
00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:40.157
Whether they recognised them or not, the scenes still worked.

58
00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.605
Whether a reference is mockery or homage often depends on tone.

59
00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:57.048
'Whether X or Y' means 'in either case' and the main clause stays true. 'Whether … or not' covers both possibilities. A 'whether' clause can also be the subject: 'Whether it works depends on tone.'

60
00:03:57.783 --> 00:04:02.723
Be careful! Don't say: Whether through allusion and parody, texts echo.

61
00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:07.827
Say: Whether through allusion or parody, texts echo.

62
00:04:08.127 --> 00:04:09.848
The pattern is 'whether … or …'.

63
00:04:10.633 --> 00:04:13.262
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:15.706
No Text Is an Island.

65
00:04:16.006 --> 00:04:22.987
The term intertextuality, introduced by Julia Kristeva, suggests that every text is built from earlier texts.

66
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.217
Whether writers intend it or not, their work absorbs and transforms what they have read.

67
00:04:29.233 --> 00:04:35.714
Some references are explicit quotations, whereas others are faint echoes that only experienced readers detect.

68
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:41.161
Parody and homage both depend on recognition, although one mocks while the other honours.

69
00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:46.697
Whether we notice them or not, these connections give familiar stories their layered meaning.

70
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:52.115
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.157
blank you like it or not, the film borrows heavily.

72
00:04:56.457 --> 00:04:59.356
A, Whether. B, If. C, Either.

73
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:04.633
The answer is A: Whether.

74
00:05:05.783 --> 00:05:09.339
Whether by allusion blank parody, texts echo each other.

75
00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:13.664
A, and. B, or. C, nor.

76
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:18.863
The answer is B: or.

77
00:05:20.017 --> 00:05:22.922
A respectful tribute to an earlier work is an blank.

78
00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:26.457
A, homage. B, parody. C, echo.

79
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.023
The answer is A: homage.

80
00:05:33.183 --> 00:05:35.984
An imitation that mocks the original is a blank.

81
00:05:36.284 --> 00:05:40.216
A, reference. B, allusion. C, parody.

82
00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:45.720
The answer is C: parody.

83
00:05:46.883 --> 00:05:50.857
According to the reading, what do parody and homage both depend on?

84
00:05:51.157 --> 00:05:54.833
A, recognition. B, quotation. C, humour.

85
00:05:58.367 --> 00:06:00.773
The answer is A: recognition.

86
00:06:01.967 --> 00:06:05.072
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:05.917 --> 00:06:09.275
Can you find an allusion to another work in something you've read recently?

88
00:06:26.083 --> 00:06:27.618
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:27.918 --> 00:06:58.907
Recently I read a short story that opens with the line 'Once upon a time, there was a girl who did not want to be rescued', which is a clear allusion to traditional fairy tales. Whether the reader notices it or not, the line challenges the idea of the passive princess. The allusion gives the story layered meaning, because we compare it with stories we knew as children. Some readers might find the reference too obvious. Personally, I think its directness is part of its humour.

90
00:06:59.207 --> 00:07:00.998
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:01.883 --> 00:07:05.859
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and whetheror clauses.

92
00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:12.158
Today's value is literary awareness. Every text talks to other texts. Listen for the echoes.

93
00:07:12.458 --> 00:07:15.191
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:15.491 --> 00:07:18.562
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

